383:In previous work, our group has demonstrated that applying a threshold of FSQC &gt; 2.5 (as we have  done  in  the  principal  analyses)  substantially  attenuates  associations  between  regional cortical  measures  and  QC,  such  that  remaining  associations  are  very  small  and  far  less widespread, and that controlling for Euler number (as we have also done) further reduces these associations. We also demonstrate that case-control comparisons appear to be more reliable after either controlling for FSQC or Euler number, and that both FSQC and Euler number are highly correlated with specific measures of motion. However, to ensure that our analyses were not biassed by our specific methods of QC, we have also rerun all interaction and main effect analyses using various combinations and methods of accounting for QC, including using FSQC only as a cut off (N = 4255; without controlling for Euler number), using Euler number only as a cut off (N = 4367), and using both FSQC and Euler number as a cut off (N = 4015). The main
385:analyses were run in exactly the same way with the exception of these changes in QC method, to assess the impact of QC procedure on results. The cut off point for the Euler number was at 2 median absolute deviations, which was equivalent to a Euler number of 254. The cut off point for FSQC was 2.5, as in the main analyses.
